course: dictionary
lesson: 3135
title: Eligible: allowed by the rules
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Eligible means allowed to do or get something because you meet the rules. «Eligible to vote» means the rules say you are allowed to vote. «Eligible for free school meals» means the rules say you can get them. Look at the words around eligible to find out what the rules are.
explain: Two small words tell you a lot. «Eligible for» is followed by a thing you can get, like a prize or a meal. «Eligible to» is followed by an action you are allowed to do, like vote. To check your meaning, swap in «allowed» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Old books use eligible in a different way. In an old novel, a character might call a young man an «eligible bachelor». Here it is old-fashioned and means a good person to marry, usually rich and single. Toad, in The Wind in the Willows, describes his house like an estate agent's advert: «an eligible self-contained gentleman's residence». Grand words like this make a home sound more impressive.
fact: Eligible means allowed to do or get something because you meet the rules.
fact: «Eligible for» is followed by something you can get, such as a prize or free school meals.
fact: «Eligible to» is followed by something you are allowed to do, such as «eligible to vote».
fact: In the UK, USA, Canada and Australia, people become eligible to vote at 18.
fact: Old-fashioned meaning: a good person to marry, usually rich and single, as in «an eligible bachelor».
exercise: «Only children in Year 5 are eligible for the swimming prize.» What does eligible mean here? | Lost for ever | Very wet | Too tired to swim | *Allowed to get the prize because you meet the rule
exercise: Riya says eligible means eager. What does it really mean? | Eager and keen | *Allowed, because you meet the rules | Silly | Very tall
exercise: In «eligible to vote», what is the person allowed to do? | *Vote | Sleep | Shout | Sing
test: At what age are people in the UK eligible to vote? | Nine | Twenty-one | *Eighteen | Twelve
test: In an old novel, a character calls a young man an «eligible bachelor». What does eligible mean here? | Late for dinner | *A good, rich, single man who would make a good husband | Very tall and thin | Clever at maths
exam: Mia reads «He was eligible for the free meals.» Which swap fits best for eligible? | Sleepy | *Allowed, because he met the rules | Lost | Hungry
